@page "/sentry"

<PageTitle>Sentry · Belemetry</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Sentry</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        Sentry takes OTLP traces and logs, but nothing it receives over OTLP becomes an Issue. The Sentry exporter
        posts each error as a Sentry event to the project's envelope endpoint, so one pipeline carries traces, logs and
        metrics over OTLP and errors as Sentry Issues - grouped by their stack traces, symbolicated, and linked to
        their traces - without a Sentry SDK in the app.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> DSN</h3>
    <p>
        The exporter needs the project's DSN (<em>Settings - Client Keys</em>). Outside the browser, with
        <code>options.UseEnvironmentVariables</code> on, the DSN of an exporter added without one is read from
        <code>SENTRY_DSN</code> - but that variable never adds the exporter by itself: an app sets it for a Sentry SDK of
        its own, which would report every error a second time. An invalid DSN is skipped with a startup warning. For
        traces and logs in Sentry as well, add an OTLP exporter to the project's OTLP endpoint next to it.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@BasicCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
    <p>
        The DSN's public key is public by design - Sentry's browser SDK puts it in every page - so the browser script
        sends its JS errors to Sentry too (<code>IncludeInBrowser</code>, on by default), with the key in the URL. A
        legacy secret key in the DSN is never used, nor handed to the page. With the <a href="/transport">Relay
        transport</a> the browser's errors reach Sentry through .NET, as the same events.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> What becomes an event</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>
            A record carrying an exception - an <code>ILogger</code> entry with one, <code>TrackException</code>, an
            unhandled .NET exception, a JS error, an exception recorded on a span - becomes an error event with its
            exception chain and its stack trace.
        </li>
        <li>
            A plain log record becomes a message event. Both only at or above <code>MinimumEventLevel</code>
            (<code>Error</code> by default, as with Sentry's own logging integration). Page views, custom events and
            web vitals never do.
        </li>
        <li>
            An exception logged under a span and recorded on that span too is one event, and the error limits apply
            as to every other exporter.
        </li>
        <li>
            Each event carries its record's trace and span ids, so the Issue links to the trace Sentry received over
            OTLP, and the user (<code>user.id</code>, else <code>enduser.pseudo.id</code>), the session, the release,
            the environment and the record's other attributes - all as the privacy rules and processors left them.
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Symbolication</h3>
    <p>
        Neither Relay nor Sentry parses a stack string, so Belemetry sends the frames. A .NET frame carries the
        method's metadata token and its IL offset, and the event lists the assembly's <code>pe_dotnet</code> debug
        image: upload the PDBs, and Sentry's symbolicator adds the file and the line. That needs the assembly as a
        file - servers, desktop apps, MAUI on Windows. In WebAssembly, on Android and iOS, and in single-file or
        NativeAOT apps, the frames carry what the stack trace's text tells. JavaScript stacks are parsed for V8,
        SpiderMonkey and JavaScriptCore; upload the source maps for the release.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@UploadCode" Language="Shell" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Crashes on Android, iOS and Mac Catalyst</h3>
    <p>
        Native crashes on mobile are out of Belemetry's reach. Keep a Sentry SDK there as the crash reporter only - the
        core <code>Sentry</code> package rather than <code>Sentry.Maui</code>, with tracing off - and turn
        <code>IncludeUnhandledExceptions</code> off, as that SDK reports the .NET crashes too. Belemetry sends the rest.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@MobileCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Release, IP address and delivery</h3>
    <p>
        <code>Release</code> defaults to <code>{service.name}@@{service.version}</code>. With <code>MaskIp</code> (on by
        default) Sentry is told not to infer the user's IP address. A rate limit (429, with <code>Retry-After</code>)
        and an unavailable Relay (502, 503, 504) are retried, and kept by the <a href="/persistence">offline
        persistence</a> while the network is down; an event keeps its id across retries, so Sentry drops a duplicate.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@MoreCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> API</h3>
    <ApiTable TypeName="BelemetrySentryExporterOptions" />
</div>

@code {
    private const string BasicCode = """
        builder.Services.AddBitBelemetryServices(options =>
        {
            options.ServiceName = "shop";
            options.Environment = builder.Environment.EnvironmentName;

            // Errors as Sentry Issues.
            options.AddSentryExporter(builder.Configuration["Sentry:Dsn"]!);

            // Traces and logs in Sentry too, over OTLP: the endpoints and the header from the project's Client Keys page.
            options.AddOtlpExporter(o =>
            {
                o.TracesEndpoint = new Uri(builder.Configuration["Sentry:OtlpTracesEndpoint"]!);
                o.LogsEndpoint = new Uri(builder.Configuration["Sentry:OtlpLogsEndpoint"]!);
                o.Signals = BelemetrySignals.Traces | BelemetrySignals.Logs;
                o.BrowserHeaders["x-sentry-auth"] = "sentry sentry_key=<public key>";   // public, like the DSN's key
            });
        });
        """;

    private const string UploadCode = """
        dotnet publish -c Release
        sentry-cli debug-files upload --org <org> --project <project> --include-sources bin/Release/net10.0/publish
        """;

    private const string MobileCode = """
        options.AddSentryExporter(dsn, sentry =>
        {
        #if ANDROID || IOS || MACCATALYST
            // The Sentry SDK, as the crash reporter, reports the .NET crashes too.
            sentry.IncludeUnhandledExceptions = false;
        #endif
        });
        """;

    private const string MoreCode = """
        options.AddSentryExporter(dsn, sentry =>
        {
            sentry.MinimumEventLevel = LogLevel.Warning;   // warnings become events too
            sentry.Release = "shop@2.0.0+4f1c2e9";
            sentry.MaskIp = true;                          // the default
            sentry.Compression = BelemetryCompression.Gzip;
            sentry.IncludeInBrowser = false;               // only .NET's errors
        });
        """;
}
